WEBVTT

NOTE Captions for 'The Central Limit Theorem in 90 seconds'. Timings come from the narration track.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.275
Roll one die, and every number from one to six is equally likely. The picture is flat.

2
00:00:06.345 --> 00:00:10.730
Now roll ten dice, average them, and repeat that thousands of times.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.914
The averages pile up into a bell.

4
00:00:13.314 --> 00:00:15.398
This is the Central Limit Theorem.

5
00:00:15.468 --> 00:00:23.326
Take many independent samples from almost any population with finite variance, and the sample means become approximately normal.

6
00:00:23.396 --> 00:00:29.774
They centre on the true mean, μ, and their spread, the standard error, is σ divided by the square root of n.

7
00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:34.708
Why does this happen? In an average, high and low values cancel each other out.

8
00:00:34.778 --> 00:00:39.197
And there are far more combinations that land near the middle than at the edges.

9
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:45.725
Even strongly skewed data, like waiting times, are smoothed into a bell once the samples are large enough.

10
00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:48.327
The idea took two centuries to build.

11
00:00:48.397 --> 00:01:00.878
Abraham de Moivre found the bell in coin tosses in 1733. Laplace generalised it around 1810, Lyapunov proved it in 1901, and George Pólya named it in 1920.

12
00:01:01.278 --> 00:01:11.874
Today it sits underneath confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, opinion polls, clinical trials, A/B tests, and the control charts that keep factories on target.

13
00:01:11.944 --> 00:01:17.650
Whenever research reports an average with a margin of error, this theorem is quietly doing the work.

14
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:24.847
Just remember the conditions: independent observations, finite variance, and bigger samples for skewed data.

15
00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:28.281
Now open the lab, and watch the bell appear for yourself.
